# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: mill-subjection-1.logic.r2 has "opposed_progress"; mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.3 have "opposed_progress" and "isolated ∧ opposed_progress → discrepancy_matter"; they differ when only "the social subordination of women stands out an isolated fact in modern social institutions, a solitary breach of their fundamental law, a relic of an old world exploded in everything else" and "the nature of women's subordination is radically opposed to the progressive movement of the modern world, which has swept away everything else of an analogous character" are true, which the first allows
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the social subordination of women stands out an isolated fact in modern social institutions, a solitary breach of their fundamental law, a relic of an old world exploded in everything else" and "the nature of women's subordination is radically opposed to the progressive movement of the modern world, which has swept away everything else of an analogous character" are true and "the discrepancy between this social fact and all those which accompany it affords serious matter for reflection to a conscientious observer" is false. Is S2, "This entire discrepancy between one social fact and all those which accompany it, and the radical opposition between its nature and the progressive movement which is the boast of the modern world, and which has successively swept away everything else of an analogous character, surely affords, to a conscientious observer of human tendencies, serious matter for reflection.", true then?
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: mill-subjection-1.logic.r2 has nothing from it; mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.3 have "discrepancy_matter → unfavourable_presumption" and "unfavourable_presumption → balanced_question"; they differ when only "the discrepancy between this social fact and all those which accompany it affords serious matter for reflection to a conscientious observer" and "the discrepancy raises a prima facie presumption on the unfavourable side, outweighing any which custom and usage could create on the favourable" are true, which the first allows
mill-subjection-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the discrepancy between this social fact and all those which accompany it affords serious matter for reflection to a conscientious observer" and "the discrepancy raises a prima facie presumption on the unfavourable side, outweighing any which custom and usage could create on the favourable" are true and "the subordination of women is at least a balanced question, like the choice between republicanism and royalty" is false. Is S3, "It raises a prima facie presumption on the unfavourable side, far outweighing any which custom and usage could in such circumstances create on the favourable; and should at least suffice to make this, like the choice between republicanism and royalty, a balanced question.", true then?
mill-subjection-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-subjection-1.logic.r2 has "isolated ∧ opposed_progress → unfavourable_presumption" and "unfavourable_presumption → balanced_question"; mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-subjection-1.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "the social subordination of women stands out an isolated fact in modern social institutions, a solitary breach of their fundamental law, a relic of an old world exploded in everything else", "the nature of women's subordination is radically opposed to the progressive movement of the modern world, which has swept away everything else of an analogous character" and "the discrepancy raises a prima facie presumption on the unfavourable side, outweighing any which custom and usage could create on the favourable" are true, which the second allows
